    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_ff4c9f5d9e0631dfd646ae20e8580ba85d24f1bb401c62cc6775d85af7773ed9",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: modular conjugacy multiplicity is normalized as alpha(n)=g(n) log n/n with mean one; near-diagonal spectral pairs are modeled by the shifted mean gamma(r), whose Fourier transform f enters R_2 and K. A cited finite-modular result gives residue-class means, and a Hardy-Littlewood-style generating-function argument then models rational pole weights beta(p,q). Interpretation: normalization removes predictable growth and exposes multiplicity fluctuations, while finite residue data are used—under existence, mean replacement, continuum, distribution, Tauberian, and pole-dominance assumptions—to reconstruct a spectral correlation model. Boundary: the source assumes gamma exists, invokes the modular-distribution theorem from [23], and explicitly says 'accepting this' before the pole approximation; the route is not exact despite the section title. Conventions need the PDF.",
